      "sourceFeatureExtractionItemID": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "I forgot to mention - EasyAntiCheat is *incredibly* hostile to figuring out what went wrong even when you're trying very hard to be an obedient user.\n\nWith the current EAC userspace module approach, late 2022/early 2023, I tried to help a friend who was plagued with \"Unexpected error. (#1)\", with no other useful diagnostic logs.  VRChat support did not offer help (as Linux other than Steam Deck is not officially supported).\n\nSo, I signed up as a \"developer\" to Epic Online Services, created my own *entire EasyAntiCheat project*, and recreated the launch error issue just so I could get support from Epic Games directly.  Even then, EOS support wasn't able to figure out the issue!  It turns out their switch from 32-bit to 64-bit Windows bootstrapper just so happened to work around the launch crash.  Later, VRChat blocked updating \"start_protected_game.exe\", after which I found a Proton issue thread mentioning 32-bit bootstrapper EAC can have issues with too many cores - more than 40 threads, apparently.  There's a Wine CPU topology hiding flag.\n\nWhen EOS' fancy new anti-cheat that hooks into multiple levels of the system stumbles on something and breaks, what do we do to get support then?  Buying/building a second high-spec computer just for VR is exorbitantly expensive right now.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
