      },
      "restoredAt": null,
      "sourceFeatureExtractionItemID": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "Straight up this would be a deal breaker, unacceptable and stupid to implement.\n\nVRChat would gain nothing, its not some ranked competitive game.\n\nPeople run Linux to get AWAY from this sort of thing and to get AWAY from this Windows 11 style direction of hardware black boxes and such.\n\nDon't shoot yourself in the foot on this one :/",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
  "created": "2026-10-14T17:58:46.553Z",
  "deleteID": null,
  "deletedAt": null,
  "deletedBy": null,
  "details": "Epic Games launched EasyAntiCheat v2 for Linux, which adds a whole host of system lockdown requirements, including Secure Boot, TPM attestation/measured boot, limiting the root account with LSM, EAC's eBPF auditing code, etc:\n\nhttps://onlineservices.epicgames.com/news/introducing-easy-anti-cheat-linux-v2\n\nIt's slightly better than the Windows custom kernel module, but still a massive overreach in system control.\n\nVRChat, please don't do this.  I know you did on Windows 11, but let Linux be at least a little more free…  Part of the reason many folks are getting tired of Windows and switching to Linux is the level of \"we own your computer\" security and DRM measures put in place.\n\n(Steam Deck doesn't support EACv2 right now, but Epic Games are in active talks with Valve to \"fix\" this, so I can't rely on that long term)",
